generic SaaS aesthetics\
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0
\cf3 \strokec3 \
\pard\pardeftab720\sa321\partightenfactor0

\f0\b\fs48 \cf0 \strokec2 28. VISUAL LANGUAGE\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa240\partightenfactor0

\f1\b0\fs24 \cf0 Use:\
generous whitespace\
soft shadows\
subtle borders\
rounded cards\
clean typography\
modern real-estate photography\
soft pastel backgrounds\
simple line icons\
calm financial dashboards\
The interface should feel:\
trustworthy\
modern\
accessible\
professional\
calm\
premium without being luxurious\
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0
\cf3 \strokec3 \
\pard\pardeftab720\sa321\partightenfactor0

\f0\b\fs48 \cf0 \strokec2 29. HOMEPAGE\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa240\partightenfactor0

\f1\b0\fs24 \cf0 Maintain the reference structure.\
Hero:\
"Own a piece of real estate.\
Build your future."\
Supporting explanation.\
Primary CTA:\
Start Investing\
Secondary CTA:\
How It Works\
Then:\
Featured Properties\
Why RREstate\
Low Entry Barrier\
Transparent & Secure\
Rental Income\
Global Opportunities\
Then final CTA.\
Do not overpopulate the homepage.\
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0
\cf3 \strokec3 \
\pard\pardeftab720\sa321\partightenfactor0

\f0\b\fs48 \cf0 \strokec2 30. PORTFOLIO\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa240\partightenfactor0

\f1\b0\fs24 \cf0 The portfolio is one of the most important pages.\
It should immediately answer:\
What have I invested?\
What properties do I participate in?\
How much have I invested?\
What distributions have I received?\
What is my current portfolio value?\
How is my portfolio performing?\
Main sections:\
My Portfolio\
Total Invested\
Total Distributions\
Current Portfolio Value\
Portfolio Allocation\
Portfolio Performance\
My Properties\
Recent Transactions\
Recent Distributions\
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0
\cf3 \strokec3 \
\pard\pardeftab720\sa321\partightenfactor0

\f0\b\fs48 \cf0 \strokec2 31. PROPERTY PAGE\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa240\partightenfactor0

\f1\b0\fs24 \cf0 Each property detail page should include:\
hero image\
location\
property title\
investment status\
funding progress\
property value\
minimum investment\
expected yield\
financial overview\
rental information\
expenses\
investment structure\
risk information\
documents\
photos\
location\
important disclosures\
CTA to invest where legally and technically appropriate.\
Do not promise returns.\
Clearly distinguish:\
projected\
estimated\
historical\
actual\
guaranteed\
Only use "guaranteed" when legally and factually true.\
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0
\cf3 \strokec3 \
\pard\pardeftab720\sa321\partightenfactor0

\f0\b\fs48 \cf0 \strokec2 32. INVESTMENT FLOW\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa240\partightenfactor0

\f1\b0\fs24 \cf0 The investment process must be explicit.\
Conceptual flow:\
User views property\
\uc0\u8595 \
User selects investment amount\
\uc0\u8595 \
Frontend validates input\
\uc0\u8595 \
Backend validates authentication\
\uc0\u8595 \
Backend validates authorization\
\uc0\u8595 \
Backend validates investment opportunity status\
\uc0\u8595 \
Backend validates minimum amount\
\uc0\u8595 \
Backend validates currency\
\uc0\u8595 \
Backend validates available capacity\
\uc0\u8595 \
Backend creates idempotent investment intent\
\uc0\u8595 \
Payment process begins\
\uc0\u8595 \
Payment result is verified server-side\
\uc0\u8595 \
Investment becomes confirmed\
\uc0\u8595 \
Transaction recorded\
\uc0\u8595 \
Investor position updated\
\uc0\u8595 \
Portfolio updated\
\uc0\u8595 \
Audit event recorded\
\uc0\u8595 \
User notified\
Never mark an investment as paid because the frontend says payment succeeded.\
Payment confirmation must come from the trusted payment provider/backend flow.\
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0
\cf3 \strokec3 \
\pard\pardeftab720\sa321\partightenfactor0

\f0\b\fs48 \cf0 \strokec2 33. DISTRIBUTION FLOW\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa240\partightenfactor0

\f1\b0\fs24 \cf0 A distribution should follow a controlled process.\
Example:\
Property generates rental income\
\uc0\u8595 \
Income recorded\
\uc0\u8595 \
Expenses recorded\
\uc0\u8595 \
Net distributable amount calculated\
\uc0\u8595 \
Distribution created\
\uc0\u8595 \
Eligible investor positions calculated\
\uc0\u8595 \
Distribution allocations created\
\uc0\u8595 \
Review/approval\
\uc0\u8595 \
Payment initiated\
\uc0\u8595 \
Payment confirmed\
\uc0\u8595 \
Investor transaction recorded\
\uc0\u8595 \
Portfolio updated\
\uc0\u8595 \
Audit event recorded\
\uc0\u8595 \
Investor notified\
Every step must be traceable.\
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0
\cf3 \strokec3 \
\pard\pardeftab720\sa321\partightenfactor0

\f0\b\fs48 \cf0 \strokec2 34. AUDIT LOGGING\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa240\partightenfactor0

\f1\b0\fs24 \cf0 Financial and security-sensitive operations must be auditable.\
Record events such as:\
login\
failed login\
password change\
investment creation\
payment status change\
investment confirmation\
distribution creation\
distribution approval\
document access\
admin modification\
property modification\
role modification\
Do not store secrets in audit logs.\
Audit logs should be append-oriented.\
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0
\cf3 \strokec3 \
\pard\pardeftab720\sa321\partightenfactor0

\f0\b\fs48 \cf0 \strokec2 35. ADMIN PANEL\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa240\partightenfactor0

\f1\b0\fs24 \cf0 Create a secure admin area.\
Administrators should eventually be able to manage:\
users\
properties\
investment opportunities\
documents\
investment status\
distributions\
notifications\
risk disclosures\
legal documents\
company funding rounds\
audit logs\
Do not allow frontend-only admin protection.\
Every admin endpoint must be protected server-side.\
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0
\cf3 \strokec3 \
\pard\pardeftab720\sa321\partightenfactor0

\f0\b\fs48 \cf0 \strokec2 36. OBSERVABILITY\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa240\partightenfactor0

\f1\b0\fs24 \cf0 Use Spring Boot Actuator.\
Expose only appropriate production endpoints.\
Include:\
health\
readiness\
liveness\
metrics where appropriate\
Do not expose sensitive environment/configuration information publicly.\
Use structured logging.\
Include request/correlation IDs.\
Do not log sensitive data.\
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0
\cf3 \strokec3 \
\pard\pardeftab720\sa321\partightenfactor0

\f0\b\fs48 \cf0 \strokec2 37. HEALTH CHECKS\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa240\partightenfactor0

\f1\b0\fs24 \cf0 The Docker environment must support health checks.\
Backend health check.\
Database health check.\
Frontend/reverse-proxy health check if applicable.\
Application startup should fail clearly if required configuration is missing.\
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0
\cf3 \strokec3 \
\pard\pardeftab720\sa321\partightenfactor0

\f0\b\fs48 \cf0 \strokec2 38. DOCKER\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa240\partightenfactor0

\f1\b0\fs24 \cf0 The application must be Docker-ready from the beginning.\
Use multi-stage builds.\
Do not ship development dependencies unnecessarily.\
Do not run application containers as root unless technically unavoidable.\
Use minimal runtime images where practical.\
Pin important image versions rather than blindly using latest.\
Example architecture:\
frontend container\
backend container\
postgres container\
reverse proxy container\
Do not expose PostgreSQL publicly.\
Only expose the necessary public ports.\
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0
\cf3 \strokec3 \
\pard\pardeftab720\sa321\partightenfactor0

\f0\b\fs48 \cf0 \strokec2 39. DOCKER COMPOSE\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa240\partightenfactor0

\f1\b0\fs24 \cf0 Maintain:\
compose.yaml\
for development/base configuration.\
Maintain:\
compose.production.yaml\
for production overrides.\
Production should not bind-mount application source code.\
Use production-specific:\
environment variables\
restart policies\
resource configuration\
networking\
volumes\
health checks\
Docker explicitly supports this base + production override approach.\
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0
\cf3 \strokec3 \
\pard\pardeftab720\sa321\partightenfactor0

\f0\b\fs48 \cf0 \strokec2 40. REVERSE PROXY\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa240\partightenfactor0

\f1\b0\fs24 \cf0 Use a reverse proxy such as Caddy or Nginx.\
Preferred simple production architecture:\
Internet\
\uc0\u8595 \
HTTPS\
\uc0\u8595 \
Reverse Proxy\
\uc0\u9500 \u9472 \u9472  /\
\uc0\u9474  frontend\
\uc0\u9474 \
\uc0\u9492 \u9472 \u9472  /api\
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0

\f2\fs26 \cf0 backend\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa240\partightenfactor0

\f1\fs24 \cf0 The frontend and backend should appear under one primary domain where practical.\
Example:\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa240\partightenfactor0
{\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://rrestate.com/"}}{\fldrslt \cf4 \ul \ulc4 \strokec4 https://rrestate.com}}\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa240\partightenfactor0
{\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://rrestate.com/api/v1/"}}{\fldrslt \cf4 \ul \ulc4 \strokec4 https://rrestate.com/api/v1/}}...\
This simplifies CORS and deployment.\
If a separate API subdomain is necessary:\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa240\partightenfactor0
{\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://api.rrestate.com/"}}{\fldrslt \cf4 \ul \ulc4 \strokec4 https://api.rrestate.com}}\
configure CORS explicitly.\
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0
\cf3 \strokec3 \
\pard\pardeftab720\sa321\partightenfactor0

\f0\b\fs48 \cf0 \strokec2 41. HTTPS\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa240\partightenfactor0

\f1\b0\fs24 \cf0 Production must use HTTPS.\
HTTP should redirect to HTTPS.\
Cookies must use:\
Secure\
HttpOnly\
SameSite\
as appropriate.\
Do not run production authentication over plain HTTP.\
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0
\cf3 \strokec3 \
\pard\pardeftab720\sa321\partightenfactor0

\f0\b\fs48 \cf0 \strokec2 42. ENVIRONMENT CONFIGURATION\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa240\partightenfactor0

\f1\b0\fs24 \cf0 Create separate configurations:\
development\
test\
staging\
production\
Example backend:\
application.yml\
application-dev.yml\
application-test.yml\
application-prod.yml\
Never commit production secrets.\
Provide:\
.env.example\
but never:\
.env\
with real credentials.\
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0
\cf3 \strokec3 \
\pard\pardeftab720\sa321\partightenfactor0

\f0\b\fs48 \cf0 \strokec2 43. SECRETS\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa240\partightenfactor0

\f1\b0\fs24 \cf0 Secrets must come from the deployment environment or secure secret-management system.\
Never hard-code:\
database passwords\
JWT private keys\
payment secrets\
email passwords\
cloud credentials\
API keys\
encryption keys\
Do not put them into:\
Git\
Dockerfile\
frontend bundle\
VITE_* variables\
source code\
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0
\cf3 \strokec3 \
\pard\pardeftab720\sa321\partightenfactor0

\f0\b\fs48 \cf0 \strokec2 44. CI/CD\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa240\partightenfactor0

\f1\b0\fs24 \cf0 Create a GitHub Actions pipeline.\
At minimum:\
Pull request:\
frontend lint\
frontend typecheck\
frontend tests\
frontend build\
backend tests\
backend integration tests\
backend build\
Docker build validation\
Main branch:\
all checks\
build production images\
publish images\
deploy to server\
Run database migrations safely during deployment.\
Do not deploy code that fails tests.\
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0
\cf3 \strokec3 \
\pard\pardeftab720\sa321\partightenfactor0

\f0\b\fs48 \cf0 \strokec2 45. DEPLOYMENT TARGET\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa240\partightenfactor0

\f1\b0\fs24 \cf0 The application should be deployable to a single Linux VPS initially.\
For example:\
Hetzner\
Ubuntu LTS\
Docker\
Docker Compose\
Caddy/Nginx\
PostgreSQL\
This is intentionally simple.\
Do NOT introduce Kubernetes unless there is a demonstrated need.\
The architecture should remain portable enough to move to managed cloud infrastructure later.\
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0
\cf3 \strokec3 \
\pard\pardeftab720\sa321\partightenfactor0

\f0\b\fs48 \cf0 \strokec2 46. BACKUPS\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa240\partightenfactor0

\f1\b0\fs24 \cf0 PostgreSQL production data must have automated backups.\
Do not consider Docker volumes alone to be a backup strategy.\
Implement/document:\
database backup\
backup retention\
restore procedure\
off-server backup\
backup verification\
A backup that has never been restored/tested should not be considered reliable.\
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0
\cf3 \strokec3 \
\pard\pardeftab720\sa321\partightenfactor0

\f0\b\fs48 \cf0 \strokec2 47. DATABASE SECURITY\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa240\partightenfactor0

\f1\b0\fs24 \cf0 PostgreSQL should:\
NOT be publicly exposed.\
Use a private Docker network.\
Use a dedicated application database user.\
Use strong credentials.\
Use least privilege.\
Production credentials must come from environment/secret storage.\
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0
\cf3 \strokec3 \
\pard\pardeftab720\sa321\partightenfactor0

\f0\b\fs48 \cf0 \strokec2 48. CORS\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa240\partightenfactor0

\f1\b0\fs24 \cf0 Development:\
localhost frontend \uc0\u8594  localhost backend\
Production:\
only explicitly configured trusted origin(s).\
Never use:\
allowedOrigins("*")\
with credentialed authentication.\
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0
\cf3 \strokec3 \
\pard\pardeftab720\sa321\partightenfactor0

\f0\b\fs48 \cf0 \strokec2 49. CSRF\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa240\partightenfactor0

\f1\b0\fs24 \cf0 Choose the CSRF strategy based on the authentication architecture.\
If authentication uses cookies for state-changing requests, implement appropriate CSRF protection.\
Do not simply disable CSRF because "this is an API."\
Document the decision.\
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0
\cf3 \strokec3 \
\pard\pardeftab720\sa321\partightenfactor0

\f0\b\fs48 \cf0 \strokec2 50. RATE LIMITING\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa240\partightenfactor0

\f1\b0\fs24 \cf0 Protect sensitive endpoints such as:\
registration\
login\
password reset\
investment creation\
payment initiation\
document access\
Use appropriate rate limiting.\
Do not allow trivial brute-force attacks.\
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0
\cf3 \strokec3 \
\pard\pardeftab720\sa321\partightenfactor0

\f0\b\fs48 \cf0 \strokec2 51. SECURITY HEADERS\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa240\partightenfactor0

\f1\b0\fs24 \cf0 Configure appropriate headers including, where applicable:\
Content-Security-Policy\
X-Content-Type-Options\
Referrer-Policy\
Permissions-Policy\
Strict-Transport-Security in production\
Do not blindly copy an incompatible CSP.\
Test the frontend after enabling it.\
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0
\cf3 \strokec3 \
\pard\pardeftab720\sa321\partightenfactor0

\f0\b\fs48 \cf0 \strokec2 52. INPUT VALIDATION\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa240\partightenfactor0

\f1\b0\fs24 \cf0 Every externally supplied value is untrusted.\
Validate:\
JSON\
query parameters\
path variables\
multipart uploads\
financial values\
IDs\
pagination\
sorting\
filtering\
Never trust:\
frontend validation\
hidden fields\
disabled buttons\
client-side roles\
client-side ownership information\
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0
\cf3 \strokec3 \
\pard\pardeftab720\sa321\partightenfactor0

\f0\b\fs48 \cf0 \strokec2 53. PAGINATION\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa240\partightenfactor0

\f1\b0\fs24 \cf0 Never return unlimited database records.\
Use pagination for:\
properties\
transactions\
distributions\
notifications\
admin tables\
audit logs\
Define sensible maximum page sizes.\
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0
\cf3 \strokec3 \
\pard\pardeftab720\sa321\partightenfactor0

\f0\b\fs48 \cf0 \strokec2 54. DATABASE PERFORMANCE\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa240\partightenfactor0

\f1\b0\fs24 \cf0 Use indexes based on actual query patterns.\
Likely indexed fields include:\
user_id\
property_id\
investment_opportunity_id\
status\
created_at\
transaction references\
foreign keys\
Do not create indexes blindly.\
Avoid N+1 queries.\
Use projections/fetch strategies when appropriate.\
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0
\cf3 \strokec3 \
\pard\pardeftab720\sa321\partightenfactor0

\f0\b\fs48 \cf0 \strokec2 55. CONCURRENCY\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa240\partightenfactor0

\f1\b0\fs24 \cf0 Investment capacity is a concurrency-sensitive operation.\
If a property has:\
$10,000\
remaining investment capacity\
two users must not simultaneously invest $8,000 and $8,000 and produce:\
$16,000 invested.\
Use appropriate database transaction boundaries and locking/atomic update strategies.\
Financial correctness is more important than micro-optimizing throughput.\
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0
\cf3 \strokec3 \
\pard\pardeftab720\sa321\partightenfactor0

\f0\b\fs48 \cf0 \strokec2 56. TRANSACTION BOUNDARIES\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa240\partightenfactor0

\f1\b0\fs24 \cf0 Financial operations must be transactional.\
Use Spring transactions deliberately.\
Do not make external payment calls inside a long database transaction unless there is a clear reason.\
Design payment state machines properly.\
Expect retries.\
Expect duplicate callbacks.\
Expect provider delays.\
Expect failures.\
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0
\cf3 \strokec3 \
\pard\pardeftab720\sa321\partightenfactor0

\f0\b\fs48 \cf0 \strokec2 57. WEBHOOKS\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa240\partightenfactor0

\f1\b0\fs24 \cf0 Payment-provider webhooks must be:\
authenticated\
validated\
idempotent\
logged\
safe to retry\
Never trust an unsigned webhook.\
Never process the same webhook twice.\
Store provider event IDs where appropriate.\
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0
\cf3 \strokec3 \
\pard\pardeftab720\sa321\partightenfactor0

\f0\b\fs48 \cf0 \strokec2 58. API DOCUMENTATION\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa240\partightenfactor0

\f1\b0\fs24 \cf0 Maintain OpenAPI documentation.\
The API documentation must reflect the real implementation.\
Do not allow documentation to become stale.\
If possible, generate frontend API types from the API contract rather than manually duplicating everything.\
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0
\cf3 \strokec3 \
\pard\pardeftab720\sa321\partightenfactor0

\f0\b\fs48 \cf0 \strokec2 59. TESTING STRATEGY\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa240\partightenfactor0

\f1\b0\fs24 \cf0 Write tests at multiple levels.\
Backend:\
unit tests\
service tests\
repository tests where useful\
controller/API tests\
security tests\
integration tests\
database integration tests\
financial calculation tests\
concurrency-sensitive tests\
Frontend:\
component tests\
form validation tests\
API-state tests\
critical user-flow tests\
At minimum, test:\
registration\
login\
authorization\
property retrieval\
investment validation\
investment creation\
duplicate investment request\
payment failure\
payment success\
distribution calculation\
portfolio calculation\
admin access\
user isolation\
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0
\cf3 \strokec3 \
\pard\pardeftab720\sa321\partightenfactor0

\f0\b\fs48 \cf0 \strokec2 60. TESTCONTAINERS\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa240\partightenfactor0

\f1\b0\fs24 \cf0 Use Testcontainers for PostgreSQL integration testing.\
Do not assume:\
"H2 behaves like PostgreSQL."\
Production database behavior should be tested against PostgreSQL.\
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0
\cf3 \strokec3 \
\pard\pardeftab720\sa321\partightenfactor0

\f0\b\fs48 \cf0 \strokec2 61. FINANCIAL TESTS\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa240\partightenfactor0

\f1\b0\fs24 \cf0 Financial calculations require particularly strong tests.\
Test:\
rounding\
currency\
percentage calculations\
minimum investment\
maximum investment\
remaining property capacity\
distribution allocation\
partial distributions\
refunds\
failed payments\
duplicate callbacks\
concurrent investment attempts\
Use fixed deterministic values.\
Never rely on floating-point approximations.\
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0
\cf3 \strokec3 \
\pard\pardeftab720\sa321\partightenfactor0

\f0\b\fs48 \cf0 \strokec2 62. FRONTEND ERROR STATES\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa240\partightenfactor0

\f1\b0\fs24 \cf0 Every important API operation must have:\
loading\
success\
empty\
error\
retry\
states where appropriate.\
Do not leave users staring at blank screens.\
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0
\cf3 \strokec3 \
\pard\pardeftab720\sa321\partightenfactor0

\f0\b\fs48 \cf0 \strokec2 63. FRONTEND SECURITY\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa240\partightenfactor0

\f1\b0\fs24 \cf0 Never assume frontend route protection is security.\
For example:\
/admin\
being hidden from a normal user does NOT secure the admin API.\
Backend authorization is authoritative.\
Never place sensitive secrets in the frontend.\
Never trust client-provided investment ownership percentages.\
Never calculate authoritative financial balances exclusively in the browser.\
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0
\cf3 \strokec3 \
\pard\pardeftab720\sa321\partightenfactor0

\f0\b\fs48 \cf0 \strokec2 64. FRONTEND DESIGN RULE\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa240\partightenfactor0

\f1\b0\fs24 \cf0 The provided RREstate mockup is the visual source of truth.\
Preserve:\
palette\
typography\
spacing\
card style\
button style\
icon style\
photography style\
dashboard structure\
visual hierarchy\
Use the same design language on:\
homepage\
properties\
property details\
login\
registration\
portfolio\
transactions\
distributions\
documents\
settings\
admin\
Do not allow the application to slowly become a generic shadcn dashboard.\
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0
\cf3 \strokec3 \
\pard\pardeftab720\sa321\partightenfactor0

\f0\b\fs48 \cf0 \strokec2 65. ACCESSIBILITY\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa240\partightenfactor0

\f1\b0\fs24 \cf0 Build accessible UI.\
Use:\
semantic HTML\
keyboard navigation\
visible focus states\
ARIA only where appropriate\
sufficient contrast\
accessible form labels\
accessible errors\
screen-reader-friendly controls\
Do not sacrifice accessibility for visual design.\
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0
\cf3 \strokec3 \
\pard\pardeftab720\sa321\partightenfactor0

\f0\b\fs48 \cf0 \strokec2 66. RESPONSIVENESS\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa240\partightenfactor0

\f1\b0\fs24 \cf0 Desktop is important.\
Mobile is equally important.\
Every major page must work on:\
mobile\
tablet\
desktop\
large desktop\
Do not simply shrink desktop layouts.\
Design responsive behavior intentionally.\
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0
\cf3 \strokec3 \
\pard\pardeftab720\sa321\partightenfactor0

\f0\b\fs48 \cf0 \strokec2 67. PERFORMANCE\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa240\partightenfactor0

\f1\b0\fs24 \cf0 Frontend:\
code splitting where useful\
lazy loading for large pages\
optimized images\
avoid unnecessary rerenders\
TanStack Query caching\
reasonable bundle size\
Backend:\
pagination\
database indexes\
connection pool configuration\
avoid N+1 queries\
cache only when justified\
Do not prematurely optimize.\
Measure before introducing complexity.\
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0
\cf3 \strokec3 \
\pard\pardeftab720\sa321\partightenfactor0

\f0\b\fs48 \cf0 \strokec2 68. LOGGING\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa240\partightenfactor0

\f1\b0\fs24 \cf0 Use structured logs.\
Logs should answer:\
what happened?\
when?\
where?\
which request?\
which user, when safe and appropriate?\
what failed?\
Never log:\
passwords\
tokens\
private keys\
full payment credentials\
sensitive identity documents\
unnecessary PII\
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0
\cf3 \strokec3 \
\pard\pardeftab720\sa321\partightenfactor0

\f0\b\fs48 \cf0 \strokec2 69. REQUEST CORRELATION\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa240\partightenfactor0

\f1\b0\fs24 \cf0 Every request should have a correlation/request ID.\
Propagate it through relevant backend logs.\
When an error occurs, the frontend should receive a safe error reference/correlation ID where useful.\
This makes production debugging significantly easier.\
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0
\cf3 \strokec3 \
\pard\pardeftab720\sa321\partightenfactor0

\f0\b\fs48 \cf0 \strokec2 70. API + FRONTEND CONTRACT DISCIPLINE\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa240\partightenfactor0

\f1\b0\fs24 \cf0 Whenever backend changes:\
CHECK FRONTEND.\
Whenever frontend expects a new API:\
CHECK BACKEND.\
Whenever database changes:\
CHECK SERVICE LAYER.\
Whenever domain logic changes:\
CHECK TESTS.\
Whenever authentication changes:\
CHECK FRONTEND SESSION/TOKEN FLOW.\
Whenever deployment configuration changes:\
CHECK DOCKER + CI/CD.\
Never treat a change as isolated.\
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0
\cf3 \strokec3 \
\pard\pardeftab720\sa321\partightenfactor0

\f0\b\fs48 \cf0 \strokec2 71. NO MAGIC VALUES\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa240\partightenfactor0

\f1\b0\fs24 \cf0 Do not scatter:\
URLs\
timeouts\
limits\
financial constants\
pagination sizes\
feature flags\
across source code.\
Use configuration/constants where appropriate.\
But do not turn every number into an unnecessary environment variable.\
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0
\cf3 \strokec3 \
\pard\pardeftab720\sa321\partightenfactor0

\f0\b\fs48 \cf0 \strokec2 72. NO PREMATURE MICROSERVICES\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa240\partightenfactor0

\f1\b0\fs24 \cf0 Start as a modular monolith.\
Use clear modules/packages:\
auth\
users\
properties\
investments\
portfolio\
transactions\
distributions\
documents\
payments\
notifications\
admin\
audit\
This allows fast development and simple deployment.\
Only extract services when there is a real operational reason.\
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0
\cf3 \strokec3 \
\pard\pardeftab720\sa321\partightenfactor0

\f0\b\fs48 \cf0 \strokec2 73. DOMAIN LAYER\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa240\partightenfactor0

\f1\b0\fs24 \cf0 Business logic belongs in services/domain logic.\
Controllers should not contain complex business logic.\
Repositories should handle persistence.\
Controllers:\
HTTP\
DTOs\
validation\
response mapping\
Services:\
business rules\
transactions\
domain workflows\
Repositories:\
database access\
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0
\cf3 \strokec3 \
\pard\pardeftab720\sa321\partightenfactor0

\f0\b\fs48 \cf0 \strokec2 74. DTO MAPPING\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa240\partightenfactor0

\f1\b0\fs24 \cf0 Never expose entities directly.\
Use DTOs.\
Example:\
PropertyResponse\
InvestmentResponse\
PortfolioResponse\
TransactionResponse\
DistributionResponse\
UserResponse\
Use MapStruct where mapping becomes repetitive.\
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0
\cf3 \strokec3 \
\pard\pardeftab720\sa321\partightenfactor0

\f0\b\fs48 \cf0 \strokec2 75. API TYPES\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa240\partightenfactor0

\f1\b0\fs24 \cf0 Frontend types should correspond directly to backend API contracts.\
For example:\
Property\
Investment\
Portfolio\
Transaction\
Distribution\
User\
ErrorResponse\
Do not create conflicting representations of the same domain object without a reason.\
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0
\cf3 \strokec3 \
\pard\pardeftab720\sa321\partightenfactor0

\f0\b\fs48 \cf0 \strokec2 76. DEVELOPMENT WORKFLOW\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa240\partightenfactor0

\f1\b0\fs24 \cf0 When asked to implement a feature:\
FIRST:\
inspect the repository.\
SECOND:\
explain the affected architecture briefly.\
THIRD:\
identify required database changes.\
FOURTH:\
identify backend changes.\
FIFTH:\
identify API contract changes.\
SIXTH:\
identify frontend changes.\
SEVENTH:\
implement.\
EIGHTH:\
run tests/build.\
NINTH:\
fix errors.\
TENTH:\
summarize exactly what changed.\
Do not generate massive amounts of unrelated code.\
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0
\cf3 \strokec3 \
\pard\pardeftab720\sa321\partightenfactor0

\f0\b\fs48 \cf0 \strokec2 77. WHEN SOMETHING IS UNCLEAR\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa240\partightenfactor0

\f1\b0\fs24 \cf0 Do not invent a major architectural decision silently.\
If a decision has serious consequences:\
explain the options\
recommend one\
state the assumption\
then continue where possible.\
Do not block development over minor details.\
Prefer sensible defaults.\
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0
\cf3 \strokec3 \
\pard\pardeftab720\sa321\partightenfactor0

\f0\b\fs48 \cf0 \strokec2 78. LEGAL/COMPLIANCE BOUNDARY\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa240\partightenfactor0

\f1\b0\fs24 \cf0 This is a software architecture requirement.\
The platform may involve:\
securities\
real estate\
crowdfunding\
payments\
KYC/AML\
tax reporting\
investor eligibility\
financial promotions\
custody\
property ownership\
jurisdiction-specific regulation\
Therefore:\
DO NOT claim that the platform is legally compliant merely because the code works.\
DO NOT invent regulatory rules.\
DO NOT promise returns.\
DO NOT implement real-money investment functionality as if legal approval already exists.\
Create configurable compliance gates where necessary.\
For example:\
KYC_REQUIRED\
INVESTMENT_ENABLED\
INVESTOR_ELIGIBILITY_REQUIRED\
PAYMENTS_ENABLED\
PROPERTY_INVESTMENT_ENABLED\
These can initially be disabled while the legal structure is being finalized.\
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0
\cf3 \strokec3 \
\pard\pardeftab720\sa321\partightenfactor0

\f0\b\fs48 \cf0 \strokec2 79. MVP MODE\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa240\partightenfactor0

\f1\b0\fs24 \cf0 Build the MVP first.\
MVP should prioritize:\
authentication\
users\
properties\
property details\
investment opportunities\
portfolio\
transactions\
admin\
documents\
audit logs\
API documentation\
Docker\
database migrations\
testing\
deployment\
Do NOT initially build:\
complex recommendation engines\
microservices\
blockchain\
secondary markets\
AI everywhere\
advanced trading\
complex messaging\
unnecessary event infrastructure\
Build a strong foundation first.\
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0
\cf3 \strokec3 \
\pard\pardeftab720\sa321\partightenfactor0

\f0\b\fs48 \cf0 \strokec2 80. FUTURE ECONOMIC ANALYSIS\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa240\partightenfactor0

\f1\b0\fs24 \cf0 RREstate may eventually integrate an economic analysis engine.\
This could evaluate:\
property prices\
rental yield\
inflation\
interest rates\
GDP\
currency\
country risk\
debt\
employment\
property-market trends\
economic cycles\
The existing architecture should leave room for this.\
Do not tightly couple the core investment system to AI.\
Economic analysis should eventually be a separate module/service/domain component.\
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0
\cf3 \strokec3 \
\pard\pardeftab720\sa321\partightenfactor0

\f0\b\fs48 \cf0 \strokec2 81. FUTURE AI\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa240\partightenfactor0

\f1\b0\fs24 \cf0 AI may eventually assist with:\
property analysis\
economic analysis\
market summaries\
risk explanations\
document extraction\
investor education\
But:\
AI must never become the authoritative source of:\
account balances\
ownership\
investment amounts\
payment status\
distribution amounts\
legal eligibility\
financial transaction records\
Those must come from deterministic backend systems.\
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0
\cf3 \strokec3 \
\pard\pardeftab720\sa321\partightenfactor0

\f0\b\fs48 \cf0 \strokec2 82. DEPLOYMENT ARCHITECTURE\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa240\partightenfactor0

\f1\b0\fs24 \cf0 Target architecture:\
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0

\f2\fs26 \cf0                 INTERNET\
                    |\
                 HTTPS\
                    |\
                CADDY\
               /      \\\
              /        \\\
         FRONTEND      BACKEND\
            |             |\
            |          PostgreSQL\
            |\
         Static app\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa240\partightenfactor0

\f1\fs24 \cf0 PostgreSQL must remain private.\
Only Caddy/reverse proxy should expose public HTTP/HTTPS.\
Backend should not unnecessarily be directly exposed to the internet.\
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0
\cf3 \strokec3 \
\pard\pardeftab720\sa321\partightenfactor0

\f0\b\fs48 \cf0 \strokec2 83. CONTAINER NETWORKING\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa240\partightenfactor0

\f1\b0\fs24 \cf0 Use separate Docker networks where useful.\
Example:\
public network:\
proxy\
frontend\
backend\
private network:\
backend\
postgres\
The database should not be reachable from the public network.\
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0
\cf3 \strokec3 \
\pard\pardeftab720\sa321\partightenfactor0

\f0\b\fs48 \cf0 \strokec2 84. PRODUCTION CONTAINER RULES\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa240\partightenfactor0

\f1\b0\fs24 \cf0 Containers should:\
run as non-root where practical\
have health checks\
have restart policies\
have minimal permissions\
avoid unnecessary capabilities\
not contain secrets\
not contain source-code bind mounts\
use persistent volumes only where appropriate\
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0
\cf3 \strokec3 \
\pard\pardeftab720\sa321\partightenfactor0

\f0\b\fs48 \cf0 \strokec2 85. DATABASE MIGRATION DEPLOYMENT\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa240\partightenfactor0

\f1\b0\fs24 \cf0 Application deployment must safely handle migrations.\
Preferred sequence:\
backup\
pull new images\
run migration\
verify migration\
start/restart application\
health check\
verify application\
Do not blindly destroy and recreate production database containers.\
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0
\cf3 \strokec3 \
\pard\pardeftab720\sa321\partightenfactor0

\f0\b\fs48 \cf0 \strokec2 86. ROLLBACK\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa240\partightenfactor0

\f1\b0\fs24 \cf0 Deployment documentation must explain:\
application rollback\
database migration rollback strategy\
backup restore\
image version rollback\
Do not assume every database migration can simply be reversed.\
Prefer backward-compatible migrations where possible.\
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0
\cf3 \strokec3 \
\pard\pardeftab720\sa321\partightenfactor0

\f0\b\fs48 \cf0 \strokec2 87. VERSIONING\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa240\partightenfactor0

\f1\b0\fs24 \cf0 Pin production image versions.\
Do not deploy:\
latest\
blindly.\
Use explicit versions/tags.\
Application versions should be identifiable.\
Example:\
rrestate-backend:1.0.0\
rrestate-frontend:1.0.0\
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0
\cf3 \strokec3 \
\pard\pardeftab720\sa321\partightenfactor0

\f0\b\fs48 \cf0 \strokec2 88. DOCUMENTATION\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa240\partightenfactor0

\f1\b0\fs24 \cf0 Maintain:\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa240\partightenfactor0
{\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "http://readme.md/"}}{\fldrslt \cf4 \ul \ulc4 \strokec4 README.md}}\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa240\partightenfactor0
